<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santa María del Naranco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/848">848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Santa María del Naranco is a building on Mount Naranco above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, built as a royal hall for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-i-of-asturias">Ramiro I of Asturias</a> and dated by an inscription of 848; it was later turned into a church. A two-storey hall with barrel vaults, external loggias and carved medallions, it is the most celebrated work of Asturian pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanesque-architecture">Romanesque architecture</a>. It is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> of the Monuments of Oviedo and the Kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santa-maria-del-naranco.jpg" alt="Santa María del Naranco" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Radiostud484 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SaintMaryOfNaranco.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-i-of-asturias">Ramiro I of Asturias</a> ordered the building as a recreational palace, one of a series of royal buildings on the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, on the southern slope of Mount Naranco, about 3 kilometres from the city. It was part of a larger complex that included the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-miguel-de-lillo">San Miguel de Lillo</a>, 100 metres away. The palace had in part a religious function and was consecrated in 848; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> inscription on its altar gives the date. It lost its civil character in the 12th century, when it was converted into a church dedicated to Saint Mary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The palace stands on a rectangular plan with two floors. The low lower level has a central chamber with another on each side. A double exterior stairway leads to the upper floor, which repeats the layout: a central hall with six blind semicircular arches along the walls and a mirador at each end, reached through three arches resting on columns with helicoidal rope moulding. The barrel vault is made of tufa and held by six transverse arches resting on consoles, which correspond one to one with buttresses outside, so that the building has no wooden ceiling.</p><p>The decoration is concentrated in the hall and the miradors. It includes capitals with reliefs framed by cord decoration, of Byzantine influence, and 32 medallions of similar size, with quadrupeds, birds, bunches of grapes and fantastic animals, a style inherited from the Visigothic period. Bands above them show four figures: two carrying loads on their heads and two mounted soldiers with swords, read as a symbol either of warriors defending men of prayer or of the royal and ecclesiastical orders complementing each other. A Greek cross is carved as the emblem of the Asturian monarchy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Crónica Silense, written about 1015, marvelled at a palace without wood, vaulted below and above. Chroniclers admired its proportions, slender shapes, rich decoration and long barrel vaults, whose transverse arches did away with wooden ceilings. The solution, hesitantly tried in the Holy Chamber of the cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, fully matured here, and the building is seen as a clear precursor of Romanesque construction. It was declared a national monument on 24 January 1885 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in December 1985.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Santa María del Naranco” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
